    "description": "The Iran–Iraq War began with Iraq’s invasion of Iran in September 1980 and continued until a ceasefire took effect in August 1988. Iranian counteroffensives, subsequent fighting inside Iraq, chemical warfare, and prolonged diplomacy made it a defining conflict for both countries. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": [
      "Iran–Iraq War",
      "Khuzestan",
      "Khorramshahr",
      "United Nations",
      "Chemical weapons",
      "Military history"
    ],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T14:31:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T14:31:03Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "The Iran–Iraq War, 1980–1988 | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iraq’s invasion, Iran’s counteroffensives, chemical warfare, and the UN process that produced the August 1988 ceasefire."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "invasion-and-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An invasion and a contested frontier",
        "content": "Iraq began the war on 22 September 1980 with air attacks and a ground invasion of Iran. A central geographical issue was the Shatt al-Arab, known in Iran as the Arvand Rud, the waterway along part of the countries’ southern frontier. The conflict also developed in the unsettled regional circumstances following Iran’s revolution. Border disputes and political antagonism help explain the confrontation, but do not make the decision to invade an impersonal or inevitable event. [s1]\n\nKhuzestan, Iran’s southwestern province, became a principal battlefield. The experience of its inhabitants cannot be reduced to a simple division between Arabs and Persians: the scholarly account consulted here records that Iranian Arabs did not rise in support of the invading Iraqi forces as Saddam Hussein had anticipated. [s1]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "military-turning-point",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovery of territory and continuation of war",
        "content": "Iran’s recovery of Khorramshahr in May 1982 marked a major reversal of the invasion. Later that year, Iranian forces advanced into Iraq. This distinction is essential: repelling an invasion and continuing a war across the international frontier were different strategic phases. Iranian leaders debated the feasibility and costs of an offensive, while their conditions for a settlement went beyond a simple halt to fighting. [s1]\n\nThe change in battlefield position did not produce agreement on peace. The United Nations’ subsequent diplomatic record shows how a conflict can continue even when proposals for ending it already exist: ceasefire terms require acceptance, implementation arrangements, and confidence that the other party will comply. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "chemical-warfare",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Chemical warfare and the international response",
        "content": "Chemical weapons were a documented feature of the conflict. In Resolution 612, adopted on 9 May 1988, the UN Security Council condemned their continued use as contrary to the Geneva Protocol. It also called for strict controls on exports of chemicals suitable for producing chemical weapons. The resolution establishes an international response to prohibited warfare; its language should not be stretched into an incident-by-incident attribution or a complete accounting of victims. [s3]\n\nThat distinction matters when reading diplomatic documents. A resolution can condemn conduct and demand preventive measures without supplying the medical evidence, perpetrator findings, or comprehensive casualty figures needed for a detailed history of individual attacks. [s3]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ceasefire-and-withdrawal",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ending the fighting required a separate process",
        "content": "Security Council Resolution 598 was adopted on 20 July 1987, but the ceasefire took effect on 20 August 1988. These dates describe different developments and should not be treated as interchangeable endpoints. The United Nations Iran–Iraq Military Observer Group, or UNIIMOG, was established to verify and supervise the cessation of hostilities and withdrawal toward the internationally recognized boundary. [s2]\n\nWithdrawal remained a separate implementation problem after the guns fell silent. The UN account describes forces still requiring adjustment around the border during the later monitoring period. The useful conclusion is therefore more precise than saying that a resolution ended the war: diplomacy produced a framework, the parties accepted a ceasefire, and observers then monitored practical compliance. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ceasefire-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From a Security Council framework to monitored implementation",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"20 July 1987: Resolution 598 adopted\"] -->|\"Framework for a negotiated end to fighting\"| B[\"Parties agree to ceasefire arrangements\"]\n  B -->|\"Observer mission established\"| C[\"9 August 1988: Resolution 619 establishes UNIIMOG\"]\n  C -->|\"Observers deployed for monitoring\"| D[\"20 August 1988: ceasefire takes effect\"]\n  D -->|\"Verification and supervision\"| E[\"Ceasefire and border withdrawal monitored\"]\n```",
        "summary": "Adopting a diplomatic framework, commencing a ceasefire, and verifying withdrawal were distinct steps. UNIIMOG monitored implementation; it did not itself negotiate away every underlying dispute.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": [
          "s2"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
